          "id": "sec-1-2",
          "secNumber": "1.2",
          "heading": "Unit application and simulation protocol",
          "content": "## Unit application and simulation protocol\n\n### Laboratory 01 \u2014 Basin Water Balance\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** \u0394S = P \u2212 ET \u2212 Q. Inputs are precipitation in mm/yr, evapotranspiration in mm/yr, and runoff depth in mm/yr. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Precipitation = 1200 mm/yr, Evapotranspiration = 650 mm/yr, Runoff depth = 420 mm/yr. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 900 mm/yr and up to 1800 mm/yr, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** A closed catchment accounting exercise assuming no lateral groundwater import or export and no other withdrawals. The residual is a storage change or an unclosed balance, not automatically usable supply. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** If precipitation increases by 10% with all other terms fixed, should the residual increase by precisely that volume? What physical feedbacks would invalidate holding evapotranspiration and runoff fixed? Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project.\n### Laboratory 02 \u2014 Demand Growth & Capacity\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** D(t) = D\u2080 (1+g)^t. Inputs are current demand in ML/day, annual growth in %/yr, and planning horizon in years. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Current demand = 35 ML/day, Annual growth = 2.2 %/yr, Planning horizon = 20 years. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 10 ML/day and up to 80 ML/day, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** The growth rate and per-capita use are held constant; demand projections are planning scenarios, not demographic forecasts. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** How does reducing leaks alter the baseline independently of population growth, and why can peak-day capacity be more critical than annual demand? Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project."
