          "secNumber": "2.2",
          "heading": "Unit application and simulation protocol",
          "content": "## Unit application and simulation protocol\n\n### Laboratory 03 \u2014 Allocation under Minimums\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** Essential served = min(A,E); optional served = min(max(A\u2212E,0), R). Inputs are available water in ML/day, essential minimum in ML/day, and other requests in ML/day. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Available water = 70 ML/day, Essential minimum = 25 ML/day, Other requests = 65 ML/day. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 20 ML/day and up to 110 ML/day, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** A single-period priority allocation assuming all water is physically interchangeable, with no spatial conveyance, quality or institutional restrictions. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** At which supply value does the essential minimum become unmet? Explain why the priority rule must be established outside the numerical model. Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project.\n### Laboratory 04 \u2014 Competing Demand Scenarios\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** Municipal first; remaining supply distributed to agriculture. Inputs are supply in ML/day, agriculture requested in ML/day, and municipal minimum in ML/day. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Supply = 75 ML/day, Agriculture requested = 60 ML/day, Municipal minimum = 30 ML/day. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 25 ML/day and up to 120 ML/day, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** The simplified rule is not a general basin optimizer and does not establish water rights. All demands are treated as withdrawals with no return flows. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** Which unmet demand rises first when supply falls? What would happen if ecological flows were a binding constraint? Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project."
