          "content": "## Applied project\n\n### Project 03 \u2014 Reservoir Systems: basin decision memorandum\n\n**Decision setting.** A synthetic 30-day inflow sequence contains six high-flow days followed by a long dry spell. Operate the same reservoir under a high conservation target and a more aggressive pre-release rule. Compare the peak spill, unmet downstream demand, days below reserve storage and total evaporation. The second rule may reduce flood risk but worsen dry-season supply. Note how the conclusion depends on the quality and lead time of weather forecasts.\n\n**Project mandate.** Assume a local water commission must make a reversible decision within a limited financial and operational envelope. The project is not to design a real structure; it is to demonstrate reproducible calculations and show when the engineering team must request additional information. Assign a stakeholder team to challenge the baseline scenario and a second team to challenge the preferred intervention. Students should explicitly document disagreement rather than forcing the same preference through all models.\n\n**Required tasks.**\n\n1. Define the physical system boundary and provide a map or schematic of inflow, outflow, demands and stores.\n2. Write an auditable water balance and an explicit list of observations needed to populate it.\n3. Compare a baseline, a dry-period stress case and a governance or engineering intervention.\n4. Quantify a service-reliability indicator together with one ecological or distributional consequence.\n5. State at least two risks not represented by the introductory classroom models.\n6. Propose a monitoring plan with observations, frequency, responsible parties and decision triggers.\n7. Prepare a transparent summary for people affected upstream and downstream, separating confirmed evidence from assumptions.\n\n**Method.** Investigate all four syllabus topics of Unit 3: Storage, Operation, Flood Control, Sedimentation. Use the associated pair of simulations as teaching tools, independently verify at least three quantitative results, and do not insert those simulation outputs directly into a permit or construction drawing. When two assumptions point to opposite decisions, describe the range of plausible outcomes and identify which field measurement would most cheaply reduce uncertainty. An optimized number without enforceable operational responsibilities is not a complete solution.\n\n**Submission rubric.** Technical closure (25%), evidence quality and assumptions (20%), scenario sensitivity and uncertainty (20%), environmental and equity impacts (15%), reproducibility of calculations and charts (10%), and clarity of recommendations (10%). A polished graph cannot compensate for an incorrectly defined basin boundary or missing feasibility constraint."
