          "heading": "Unit application and simulation protocol",
          "content": "## Unit application and simulation protocol\n\n### Laboratory 09 \u2014 Trapezoidal Channel Rating\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** Q = (1/n) A R^(2/3) \u221aS; A=y(b+zy), P=b+2y\u221a(1+z\u00b2). Inputs are water depth in m, bed slope in m/m, and manning n in s/m\u00b9\u141f\u00b3. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Water depth = 2 m, Bed slope = 0.001 m/m, Manning n = 0.032 s/m\u00b9\u141f\u00b3. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 0.4 m and up to 5 m, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** Trapezoid bottom width b=4 m and 1.5H:1V side slopes. Manning uniform-flow relation; no backwater, changing roughness or sediment movement. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** Is doubling depth equivalent to doubling conveyance? Calculate the changes in area and wetted perimeter. Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project.\n### Laboratory 10 \u2014 Channel Boundary Shear\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** \u03c4 = \u03c1gRS; compare \u03c4 with assumed \u03c4critical. Inputs are hydraulic radius in m, energy slope in m/m, and critical shear in Pa. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Hydraulic radius = 1.2 m, Energy slope = 0.0012 m/m, Critical shear = 17 Pa. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 0.3 m and up to 4 m, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** Mean boundary shear screening with water density 1000 kg/m\u00b3. Grain movement and bank stability require additional information beyond a single threshold. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** Why does \u03c4 above a selected threshold not by itself determine a sediment transport rate? Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project."
