          "id": "sec-6-2",
          "secNumber": "6.2",
          "heading": "Unit application and simulation protocol",
          "content": "## Unit application and simulation protocol\n\n### Laboratory 11 \u2014 Wellfield Drawdown Screen\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** s \u2248 Q/(4\u03c0T) ln(2.25Tt/(r\u00b2S)) (Cooper\u2013Jacob). Inputs are pumping rate in m\u00b3/day, transmissivity in m\u00b2/day, and radius in m. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Pumping rate = 700 m\u00b3/day, Transmissivity = 750 m\u00b2/day, Radius = 100 m. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 200 m\u00b3/day and up to 1800 m\u00b3/day, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** Illustrative confined-aquifer, constant-rate, large-time approximation with t=30 days and storativity S=0.001. Validity checked using u=r\u00b2S/(4Tt). A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** Does drawdown scale linearly with pumping? Which measured aquifer characteristics could invalidate this approximate prediction? Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project.\n### Laboratory 12 \u2014 Groundwater Storage Scenario\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** G[t+1] = max(0,G[t] + recharge\u2212withdrawal). Inputs are recharge in ML/yr, abstraction in ML/yr, and initial store in ML. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Recharge = 90 ML/yr, Abstraction = 110 ML/yr, Initial store = 850 ML. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 20 ML/yr and up to 180 ML/yr, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** A lumped groundwater bookkeeping model with ten annual steps; stream capture, lateral flow, salinity and variable recharge are omitted. Unmet abstraction is tracked. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** Can an aquifer withdraw more than annual recharge temporarily without physically violating mass balance? Why does annual equality not establish sustainability? Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project."
