          "id": "sec-8-2",
          "secNumber": "8.2",
          "heading": "Unit application and simulation protocol",
          "content": "## Unit application and simulation protocol\n\n### Laboratory 15 \u2014 Environmental Flow Allocation\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** Allowed withdrawal = min(demand,max(0,natural flow\u2212reserve)). Inputs are natural flow in ML/day, environmental reserve in ML/day, and human demand in ML/day. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Natural flow = 85 ML/day, Environmental reserve = 35 ML/day, Human demand = 65 ML/day. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 20 ML/day and up to 150 ML/day, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** A static environmental threshold is only an educational simplification. Real flow regimes use seasonal ecological objectives and local statutory requirements. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** When is the ecological reserve itself infeasible under the natural flow? How should that be reported rather than disguised as compliance? Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project.\n### Laboratory 16 \u2014 Stakeholder Weight Sensitivity\n\n**Purpose and physical meaning.** This laboratory isolates one process within water-resources engineering. It is intentionally a limited analytical or bookkeeping model rather than a digital twin of any real basin. The objective is to predict how an output responds to controlled input changes, reproduce the calculation independently and recognize the boundary beyond which the model loses credibility. Use the graph to explore variation, not as evidence of real-world accuracy.\n\n**Equation and units.** Score = normalized weighted sum of 3 illustrative criteria. Inputs are reliability weight in %, ecology weight in %, and cost score weight in %. Units matter because a factor of 1,000 can arise when litres are confused with cubic metres, or when a daily rate is used directly in a monthly balance. Write out the conversion before comparing scenarios.\n\n**Default experiment.** Start with the provided values: Reliability weight = 45 %, Ecology weight = 35 %, Cost score weight = 20 %. Write a verbal prediction of the first output. Make a hand calculation for the default setting wherever the equation permits it; for a time series, reproduce the first step and check that the total storage change equals the accumulation of fluxes. Only then compare with the displayed model output.\n\n**Sensitivity investigation.** Move the first slider down to 10 % and up to 70 %, holding the other two at their defaults. Examine the direction and shape of the response, and inspect whether the graph is discontinuous at a physical constraint such as zero storage or an allocation cap. Repeat for the second and third inputs. Export the curve to CSV and estimate a finite-difference sensitivity from adjacent rows; specify the unit of the derivative.\n\n**Boundary and validation.** Two hypothetical options have fixed synthetic 0\u2013100 scores; weight inputs are normalized to 100% rather than assumed to sum perfectly. A noncompensatory legal veto is not included. A credible validation task would compare the predicted process with a data record measured under comparable conditions and quantify deviations against uncertainties. Do not tune an input to force agreement and then claim independent verification using the same observations. Distinguish a passing arithmetic check from a validated hydrological model.\n\n**Challenge.** Find a weight combination that changes the preferred alternative. Why is a weighted score not legal permission or consensus? Report how the answer changes under an extreme but physically plausible input combination, and identify one omitted mechanism that would be important before real implementation. In your conclusion distinguish observations, model assumptions and proposed decisions. If the simulation becomes insensitive to an input, determine whether a maximum/minimum constraint has become active or an equation is coded incorrectly.\n\n**Student deliverables.** Include a hand calculation, a three-row baseline/low/high scenario table with units, the exported response plot, a one-paragraph discussion of parameter sensitivity, and a one-paragraph critique of model applicability. Name the specific monitoring records and institutional approvals a responsible engineer would seek before designing a project."
